{{// Comment: Adds an inline CSS color style to dialogue, giving each character a unique color. Enabled by default. You can enable both Dialogue Color and Visual Storytelling}}{{trim}}

{{.dialogue_colors = on}}{{trim}}
<dialogue_colors>
@Dialogue Color
(1.)
- Wrap ALL {{#if .director_state}}{{else}}{{#if .cyoa}}{{else}}{{#if .improve_input}}{{else}}non-{{user}} {{/if}}{{/if}}{{/if}}dialogue lines in inline CSS color spans, regardless of previous formatting. Follow the example EXACTLY: `<span style="color:#insert_color">"Colored dialogue example!"</span>`
{{#if .director_state}}- Give every character, including {{user}}, a distinct, consistent, readable light color that suits them; apply to newcomers{{else}}{{#if .cyoa}}- Give every character, including {{user}}, a distinct, consistent, readable light color that suits them; apply to newcomers{{else}}{{#if .improve_input}}- Give every character, including {{user}}, a distinct, consistent, readable light color that suits them; apply to newcomers{{else}}- Give every character a distinct, consistent, readable light color that suits them; apply to newcomers{{/if}}{{/if}}{{/if}}

{{// Related: Response Directives, True Thoughts}}{{trim}}
</dialogue_colors>

{{// Comment: Adds restrained animation and gradient styling to dialogue only when a line's emotional delivery calls for it. Tags are converted to animated spans by the UI regex. Enable alongside Dialogue Color when you want both effects.}}{{trim}}

{{.expressive_dialogue = on}}{{trim}}
<expressive_dialogue>
@Expressive Dialogue
- Use expressive tags only when the delivery is clear. Each tag represents a character state. Available tags: <shout>, <quiet>, <angry>, <excited>, <dizzy>, <crying>, <anxious>, <hurt>, <intoxicated>, <whispering>, <trembling>, <deadpan>
- Ordinary dialogue stays untagged; tag it only when needed
- Enclose the complete quoted line with a semantic tag. Example: <shout>"THAT WAS A TERRIBLE IDEA!"</shout>
- Keep tags around dialogue only, never narration, speaker labels, or whole paragraphs. Do not stack tags unless the story genuinely needs overlapping circumstances.
- Let the wording and punctuation carry the emotion; animation is a subtle accent, not a replacement for characterization.
{{#if .dialogue_colors}}- Dialogue Color is enabled. Nest the expressive tag around the required color span. Follow EXACTLY this template: <shout><span style="color:#insert_color">"COLORED, EMPHATIC DIALOGUE!"</span></shout>{{else}}- Follow EXACTLY this template: <tag>"Example."</tag>{{/if}}

{{// Related: Dialogue Color, Response Directives, Scene Plan}}{{trim}}
</expressive_dialogue>

